const TRV=[
[0,"Frozen Program","🧊","“Not Responding” isn’t always broken","It means the program stopped answering Windows for a moment. It may just be busy, so wait a few seconds. If it stays stuck, press Ctrl + Shift + Esc to open Task Manager and End Task."],
[0,"Unwanted Toolbar","🧲","Free installers love extras","Many free downloads bundle extra programs. Choosing Custom or Advanced install and unticking the extras can stop toolbars before they start."],
[0,"Can't Open the File","📄","The ending tells the story","The letters after the dot (.docx, .pdf, .jpg) tell Windows which program to use. If the program is missing or the ending is wrong, the file won’t open."],
[0,"Missing VCRUNTIME DLL","🧩","DLLs are shared helper files","DLL stands for Dynamic Link Library. Many programs share the same ones, like sharing tools. If one is missing, programs that need it won’t start until it is reinstalled."],
[0,"Two Antivirus Programs","🛡️","Two guards can bump into each other","Running two antivirus programs at once can make them clash and slow the PC. One trusted antivirus is the safer choice."],
[0,"Unlicensed Software Warning","🔑","Free doesn’t have to mean cracked","Cracked software often carries malware. Free and legal alternatives exist, like LibreOffice for documents."],
[0,"Update Stuck at 0%","⏳","Stuck or just busy?","Big updates can take a long time. Check that the internet works and the disk has space. Turning the PC off in the middle of an update can damage files."],
[0,"Scary Virus Pop-up","⚠️","Fake virus alerts are scams","A pop-up shouting “Your PC is infected! Call now!” is usually a scam, not real antivirus. Close it and never call the number."],
[0,"Damaged System Files","🩹","Windows can fix itself","Windows has a built-in repair tool. Running sfc /scannow in a Command Prompt opened as administrator scans system files and repairs damaged ones."],
[1,"No Internet Anywhere","📡","Modem vs router","The modem talks to your internet provider. The router shares that connection with your devices. Many home boxes do both. If every device is offline, check this box first."],
[1,"Wrong Wi-Fi Password","🔤","Passwords are case-sensitive","“Admin” and “admin” are different. One wrong capital letter can keep you offline."],
[1,"Far From the Router","🧱","Walls weaken Wi-Fi","Concrete and metal block signals the most. The 2.4 GHz band reaches farther, while 5 GHz is faster but works best at shorter range."],
[1,"IP Address Conflict","🏠","Every device needs its own address","An IP address is like a house address on the network. If two devices share one, they bump into each other. The router usually hands out addresses automatically."],
[1,"Pings Work, Websites Don't","📖","DNS is the internet’s phonebook","DNS turns names like google.com into number addresses. If DNS fails, you can still reach numbers (ping works) but not websites by name."],
[1,"Network Cable Unplugged","🔗","Cables have a length limit","A standard Ethernet cable run should be no longer than about 100 meters. The little lights on the port show when it is connected."],
[1,"School Wi-Fi Login Page","🏫","Meet the captive portal","School and café Wi-Fi often show a login page before the internet works. Open your browser and try a plain http website to make it appear."],
[1,"Slow Internet at Lunch","🍱","Everyone shares the same pipe","Your internet speed is shared by all connected devices. When many people stream or download at once, everyone slows down."],
[1,"Date Blocks Websites","📅","A wrong date can block websites","Secure websites use certificates that have dates. If your computer’s clock is wrong, the browser may refuse to open them."],
[2,"Sluggish Startup","🚀","Startup programs slow the boot","Apps set to start with Windows make the PC take longer to be ready. The Startup tab in Task Manager lets you turn off the ones you don’t need."],
[2,"Drive Almost Full","🗄️","Free space matters","Windows uses free disk space for temporary files and updates. Many technicians suggest keeping roughly 10–20% of the drive free."],
[2,"Hot and Noisy PC","🌬️","Dust is a heat trap","Dust clogs fans and vents so air can’t flow. Hot parts make the fans spin faster and louder."],
[2,"Thermal Throttling","🌡️","PCs slow down to protect themselves","When the processor gets too hot, it lowers its speed on purpose to avoid damage. That is called thermal throttling."],
[2,"Low Memory Warning","🧠","RAM is short-term memory","RAM holds what you are working on right now and clears when the PC turns off. More open apps and tabs use more of it."],
[2,"Never Restarted","🔄","Restart clears the clutter","Restarting frees memory, closes stuck background programs and finishes pending updates."],
[2,"Failing Hard Drive","💽","Clicking is a warning","Traditional hard drives have spinning disks and moving parts, while SSDs have none. Clicking sounds can mean a failing drive, so back up your files right away."],
[2,"Antivirus Scan Running","🔍","Scans use power","A full scan reads thousands of files, so the PC may feel slower until it finishes. Schedule scans for when you are not using the computer."],
[2,"Battery Saver On","🔋","Battery saver trades speed for time","It dims the screen and limits background activity, which can also lower performance, to make the battery last longer."],
[2,"Slow USB Copying","🔌","Not all USB is equal","USB 2.0 tops out at about 480 Mbps, while USB 3.0 can reach about 5 Gbps on paper. Plugging a fast drive into a slow port slows the copy."],
[2,"Recycle Bin Is Full","🗑️","Deleted isn’t gone yet","Files in the Recycle Bin still take up disk space. Emptying it frees that space."],
[3,"OS Not Found","💿","The boot order","When a PC starts, it checks devices in order to find an operating system. A flash drive left plugged in can confuse it, so unplug it and restart."],
[3,"Blue Screen After Driver Install","🟦","Drivers are translators","A driver lets Windows talk to a piece of hardware. A bad one can cause a blue screen, and Safe Mode lets you roll it back."],
[3,"Pending Updates","🔁","Updates need a restart","Many updates only finish after a restart. Ignoring “Restart required” leaves bugs and security holes open."],
[3,"Clock Keeps Resetting","🪙","A tiny battery keeps the time","The motherboard has a small coin battery, often a CR2032, that keeps the clock running when the PC is off. When it weakens, the time resets."],
[3,"Stretched Screen","🖼️","Pick the recommended resolution","Every monitor has a native resolution. Using the one marked Recommended keeps the picture sharp and in proportion."],
[3,"Forgot My Password","⌨️","Check Caps Lock first","Passwords are case-sensitive, so Caps Lock or the wrong keyboard layout can make the right password fail."],
[3,"Startup Repair Loop","🧰","Windows has a recovery toolbox","When Windows won’t start normally, the Windows Recovery Environment offers repair tools like Startup Repair and Safe Mode."],
[3,"Cannot See File Endings","🔎","Show the file endings","In File Explorer’s View settings you can turn on file name extensions. Seeing .pdf versus .exe helps you spot suspicious files."],
[3,"Permission Denied","🚫","Admin rights limit the damage","Standard accounts can’t change system-wide settings, but administrators can. This protects the PC from mistakes and malware."],
[4,"Monitor: No Signal","🖥️","“No Signal” means the screen works","The monitor is on but isn’t receiving a picture. Check the video cable at both ends and make sure the right input (HDMI or VGA) is selected."],
[4,"Printer Offline","🖨️","Offline can be just a connection","Windows marks a printer offline when it can’t reach it. Check power, the cable or Wi-Fi, and that the right printer is selected."],
[4,"Dead Wireless Mouse","🖱️","Batteries and a tiny dongle","Many wireless mice need both batteries and a small USB receiver. Check both before replacing the mouse."],
[4,"Pointer Too Fast","🎯","DPI is pointer speed","Mouse sensitivity is measured in DPI (dots per inch). A higher DPI moves the pointer farther with each movement, and Windows has its own pointer speed setting too."],
[4,"Flash Drive Not Detected","💾","Eject before you unplug","Using Safely Remove Hardware makes sure files finish writing, so your drive doesn’t get damaged."],
[4,"Faded Printing","🖨️","Faded prints have simple causes","Low ink or toner, or clogged print heads. Many printers have a head-cleaning tool in their settings."],
[4,"Unknown USB Device","🔧","Try the easy things first","USB stands for Universal Serial Bus. If Windows says unknown device, try another port or cable first, then update the driver."],
[4,"Camera Blocked","📷","Privacy comes first","Many laptops have a camera shutter or key, and Windows privacy settings control which apps can use the camera."],
[4,"Blurry Webcam","📸","Smudges blur the picture","Fingerprints and dim rooms make webcams blurry. Wipe the lens with a soft cloth and face a light."],
[4,"Bluetooth Won't Pair","📲","Pairing mode is a must","Bluetooth devices need to be in pairing mode, and many only stay connected to one device at a time."],
[4,"USB Devices Keep Disconnecting","🔋","Power saving can cut USB off","Windows can switch off USB ports to save power (selective suspend). Turning that setting off can stop devices from dropping."],
[0,"The Crashing Document","📝","One file crashes it? Check the program","If a program closes every time you open one file, the program’s own files may be damaged. Many programs, like Microsoft Office, have a Repair option that fixes them without deleting your documents."],
[0,"Missing File Error","🧩","The error names the missing piece","A message like “a required .dll file is missing” means the program lost a part. Downloading single DLLs from random sites is risky. Reinstalling with the full setup file puts the parts back safely."],
[0,"Can't Install a Program","📦","Installers need room and permission","Installing needs free disk space for the setup files and the program itself, and some installs need administrator permission. Read the message to see which one is missing."],
[0,"Can't Find My File","🗂️","Where did Save put it?","The Save As window shows the folder where the file goes, and Windows may pick Documents or OneDrive for you. File Explorer’s search box can find a file by name."],
[0,"Faulting Module Crash","📋","Crash logs name names","Event Viewer records crashes and often names the faulting module. If the same name shows up each time, look at the plugin or add-on it belongs to."],
[0,"Old Browser","🌐","Browsers need updates","Websites keep adopting new features. An old browser may not understand them, and it also misses the latest security fixes."],
[0,"Pop-up Blocked","🪟","Some logins open in a small window","Some sign-in buttons open a new small window. If the browser blocks it, it looks like nothing happened. Look for a blocked pop-up icon in the address bar."],
[0,"App Closes on Launch","💥","Trouble right after an update?","If only one app fails and it started right after an update, the update may have left the app’s files damaged. Repair, reinstall or roll back that app."],
[0,"Program Opens Very Slowly","🐢","One slow program, others fine","When only one program is slow, look at that program first. Old or damaged program files can slow it down, and updating or repairing it is a good first step."],
[0,"Cannot Open a Photo","🖼️","Photos come in many formats","JPG, PNG and HEIC are different formats. If no program is set up to open that type, Windows says so. Open with lets you pick or install one that can."],
[0,"Wrong Language in a Program","🔤","Settings control the language","Programs have a language setting that can be changed by accident. Look in the program’s Settings or Options menu, often under Language or Region."],
[0,"Report Will Not Save","💾","Saving needs free space","A “Disk is full” message means there is no room left to save. Big videos and downloads fill drives fast. Move or delete what you don’t need."],
[1,"Only My Laptop Is Offline","💻","Others online? Look at your own device","If classmates are online, the router and the internet provider are fine. The problem is on your device, like Airplane mode or Wi-Fi being turned off."],
[1,"One Website Won't Load","🗃️","Browsers keep old copies","Browsers save copies of pages, called the cache, to load faster. Old or damaged cached data can break one site on one device. Clearing the cache is a free fix."],
[1,"Weak Signal Corner","📶","Signal fades with distance","Wi-Fi gets weaker the farther you go and with every wall between you and the router. Moving closer or repositioning the router helps."],
[1,"Slow Internet While Downloading","⬇️","Big downloads hog the speed","Internet speed is shared by everyone connected. One large download can use up much of it, so pausing it or scheduling it for later helps everyone."],
[1,"Router Unplugged","🔌","No lights means no power","If all the router lights are off, start with the simplest thing: power. Check that the cable is firm and the outlet works."],
[1,"Wi-Fi Option Disappeared","🧾","Updates can swap drivers","A Windows update can replace a driver with one that doesn’t work well. In Device Manager you can roll back or reinstall the Wi-Fi driver."],
[1,"Daily Wi-Fi Drops","🍲","Microwaves and Wi-Fi share a band","Microwave ovens work near 2.4 GHz, the same band many Wi-Fi networks use, so they can cause short drops. Moving the router or using 5 GHz can help."],
[1,"Wi-Fi Icon Missing","🔘","Many laptops have a wireless key","Many laptops have a function key (Fn) or switch that turns the wireless radio on or off. Check it before trying deeper fixes."],
[1,"Login Page Not Showing","🧭","“Connected, no internet” can mean sign in","On hotspots, “no internet” often means you still have to sign in. Opening a browser and visiting a plain http website can bring up the sign-in page."],
[1,"Wrong Wi-Fi Network","🏷️","Check the network name","Guest and school networks can have similar names but different access. If a school site won’t open, check which network you joined."],
[1,"Wi-Fi Keeps Dropping","🪫","Power saving can put Wi-Fi to sleep","Windows may let the Wi-Fi card sleep to save power. In Device Manager, the adapter’s Power Management tab has a setting for this."],
[1,"Email Will Not Open","📧","Other sites work? Not the internet","If other websites load, the internet is fine. An old saved password can make just your email fail after the password was changed, so sign in again."],
[2,"Browser Overload","🗂️","Every tab uses memory","Each tab and extension uses RAM. Dozens of open tabs can slow the whole computer. Close the ones you aren’t using."],
[2,"Too Many Apps Open","🎬","Do the RAM math","Videos, documents and games each need memory. When RAM runs out, Windows leans on the much slower disk instead. Close what you don’t need."],
[2,"Runaway Process","🏃","Find the hog in Task Manager","Task Manager can sort programs by CPU use. A program stuck near 100% while you are idle, with the fan roaring, is the suspect. End Task can stop it."],
[2,"Memory Keeps Growing","📈","Beware the memory leak","A memory leak is when a program keeps grabbing memory and never gives it back. A restart clears it for a while, but updating or replacing the program fixes the cause."],
[2,"One Browser Is Slow","🧩","Add-ons add weight","Extensions run inside the browser and can slow it down, and the cache can grow large. Removing add-ons and clearing the cache often speeds it up."],
[2,"Disk at 100%","📀","Old hard drives get overwhelmed","Mechanical hard drives are slow and struggle when many programs load at once after startup. Fewer startup apps means less work for the disk."],
[2,"Slow After Many Downloads","🎮","Games are big and busy","Games take lots of space and many add background programs. Uninstalling the ones you don’t play frees space and resources."],
[2,"Video Keeps Freezing","🎞️","Video needs spare power","Playing video needs steady processor and memory. Extra tabs and programs compete for those and make the video stutter."],
[2,"Very Old Computer","🕰️","SSDs are a huge upgrade","SSDs have no moving parts and are much faster than hard drives. On an old PC, an SSD plus more RAM can make a big difference."],
[2,"Moving Wallpaper Slows PC","🌄","Pretty things cost power","Animated wallpapers and effects use processor, memory and graphics power, especially on older computers. A still picture uses almost none."],
[3,"Password Rejected","🔠","Look at the Caps Lock light","Windows passwords are case-sensitive, so Caps Lock makes a right password wrong. On some PCs, too many wrong tries can lock you out for a while."],
[3,"Whole PC Frozen","🧊","Wait first, force last","Give the PC a minute in case it is just busy. If nothing responds at all, holding the power button for several seconds forces a shutdown, but unsaved work is lost."],
[3,"Laptop Won't Turn On","🔋","Follow the power","No lights can mean an empty battery or a bad charger or outlet. Check the charger and outlet, and let it charge for a while before judging."],
[3,"Activate Windows Watermark","💧","Activation unlocks features","Windows needs a valid license. Without activation, a watermark shows and some personalization settings are locked. A valid key or digital license fixes it."],
[3,"Temporary Profile Sign-in","👤","Your profile holds your stuff","Your user profile stores your desktop, documents and settings. A bad shutdown can damage it, and Windows may sign you into a temporary profile. A restart often brings the normal one back."],
[3,"Disk Full, Few Files","🧹","Invisible files add up","Update leftovers and temporary files can take lots of space that your own folders don’t show. Disk Cleanup has a clean up system files option."],
[3,"Black Screen After Login","⬛","Explorer draws your desktop","The desktop, taskbar and Start menu are drawn by a program called Windows Explorer (explorer.exe). If it crashes, you see a black screen with a pointer. Task Manager can run it again."],
[3,"Start Menu Will Not Open","🪟","Same helper, different symptom","The Start menu is part of Windows Explorer. In Task Manager you can restart Windows Explorer to refresh it."],
[3,"Stuck on Shutting Down","⏻","Patience first","Windows may be closing programs or finishing updates. Give it a few minutes and save your work before forcing anything."],
[3,"Desktop Files Disappeared","🖥️","Every account has its own desktop","Each account has its own desktop and documents. If your files vanished, you may be signed in to a different account. Check the name shown on the Start menu."],
[3,"Laptop Sleeps Too Fast","😴","Power & sleep settings","Windows lets you choose how long until the screen turns off and the PC sleeps, separately for battery and plugged in. The time may be set too short."],
[4,"Silent Keyboard","⌨️","No lights, no power","A USB keyboard with every light off may not be getting power. Check the cable and try a different USB port."],
[4,"No Sound","🔈","Output device matters","A PC can have several outputs, like speakers, headphones and the monitor. The right one must be selected and not muted."],
[4,"Flickering Striped Screen","🦓","Swap the cheapest part first","Cables are the cheapest and most common cause of stripes. Try another cable before blaming the monitor or graphics card."],
[4,"Dead Touchpad","👆","A key can switch the touchpad off","Many laptops have a function key combination that turns the touchpad on and off. If an external mouse works, the computer itself is fine."],
[4,"Screen Flickering","📺","Reseat the connection","A loose video cable can make the screen flicker. Unplug both ends and plug them back in firmly."],
[4,"Mic Not Working","🎤","Input is not output","Output is what you hear, and input is what you say. Check the mute on the headset and choose the headset mic as the input device in Sound settings."],
[4,"Some Keys Do Not Work","🍪","Crumbs and dust","Dirt under the keys can block them. Turn the keyboard over and tap it gently, then clean around the keys with a soft brush."],
[4,"Mouse Clicks Twice","🖱️","Switches wear out","Mouse buttons use tiny switches that wear out with heavy use, causing double clicks. Cleaning may help, otherwise replace the mouse."],
[4,"Printer Prints Blank Pages","📄","Check the ink first","Empty or dried-out ink prints blank pages. Replace the cartridge and run the printer’s cleaning tool."],
[4,"Speakers Crackle","🔊","Loose plugs crackle","A loose audio plug or a very high volume can make speakers crackle. Push the plug in firmly and lower the volume."]
];
